User request:
Let $f$ be defined by  \[f(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
3-x & \text{ if } x \leq 3, \\
-x^3+2x^2+3x & \text{ if } x>3.
\end{array}
\right.\]Calculate $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6)$.

Response A:
The number $f^{-1}(0)$ is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 0$.  Since the function $f$ is defined piecewise, to find this value, we must consider both cases $x \le 3$ and $x > 3$.

If $x \le 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $3 - x = 0$, which leads to $x = 3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 0$.  This equation factors as $-x(x - 3)(x + 1) = 0$, so $x = 0$, $x = 3$, or $x = -1$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = 3$, which means $f^{-1}(0) = 3$.

Now we compute $f^{-1}(6)$, which is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 6$.

If $x \le 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $3 - x = 6$, which leads to $x = -3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 6$, or $x^3 - 2x^2 - 3x + 6 = 0$.  This equation factors as $(x - 2)(x^2 - 3) = 0$, so $x = 2$, $x = \sqrt{3}$, or $x = -\sqrt{3}$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = -3$, which means $f^{-1}(6) = -3$.

Therefore, $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6) = 3 + (-3) = \boxed{0}$.

[asy]
unitsize(3mm);
defaultpen(linewidth(.7pt)+fontsize(8pt));
import graph;

draw((-20,0)--(20,0),Arrows(4));
draw((0,-20)--(0,20),Arrows(4));

real f(real x) {return 3-x;}
real g(real x) {return -x^3+2x^2+3x;}

real x;

draw(graph(f,-15,3),BeginArrow(4));
draw(graph(g,3,4),EndArrow(4));

real eps = 0.2;

draw((-eps,3)--(eps,3));
draw((-eps,0)--(eps,0));
draw((-eps,-3)--(eps,-3));

dot("$(-3,6)$",(-3,6),SW);
dot("$(3,0)$",(3,0),NE);

label("$f(x)$",(3,20.5));
label("$x$",(20.5,-1));
[/asy]

Response B:
To find $f^{-1}(0)$, I need to solve $f(x)=0$.
There are two cases to consider: either $x \leq 3$ or $x>3$.
If $x \leq 3$, then $f(x)=3-x=0$ implies that $x=3$.
So $f^{-1}(0)=3$ in this case.
If $x>3$, then $f(x)=-x^3+2x^2+3x=0$ is a cubic equation.
I can try to factor this equation by looking for a rational root.
A rational root must be of the form $\pm p/q$, where $p$ is a factor of the constant term (0) and $q$ is a factor of the leading coefficient (-1).
So the only possible rational roots are $\pm 1$.
I can use synthetic division to test these candidates.
If I try $x=1$, I get:

\begin{array}{r|rrr}
 1 & -1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\
   &    & -1 & 1 & 4 \\ \hline
   & -1 & 1 & 4 & 4 \\
\end{array}
This means that $-x^3+2x^2+3x=(x-1)(-x^2+x+4)$.
The remainder is not zero, so $x=1$ is not a root.
If I try $x=-1$, I get:

\begin{array}{r|rrr}
 -1 & -1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\
    &    & 1 & -3 & 0 \\ \hline
    & -1 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\
\end{array}
This means that $-x^3+2x^2+3x=(x+1)(-x^2+3x)$.
The remainder is zero, so $x=-1$ is a root.
I can now factor the quadratic factor further by finding two numbers that multiply to 0 and add to 3.
The numbers are 0 and 3, so $-x^2+3x=-x(x-3)$.
Therefore, the complete factorization of $-x^3+2x^2+3x$ is $(x+1)(-x)(x-3)$.
The other two roots are $x=0$ and $x=3$.
However, these roots are not valid for the case $x>3$, so I can ignore them.
The only root that satisfies $f(x)=0$ and $x>3$ is $x=-1$.
So $f^{-1}(0)=-1$ in this case.
To find $f^{-1}(6)$, I need to solve $f(x)=6$.
